+# Scripts share git helper — plan
+intent: 2026-09-02-scripts-share-git-helper@b7b1742a8586e2862b42697c0120a0c913134da4
+
+## Current State Evidence
+- Forward: `scripts/archive.py:12` anchors `main()` calling the local `run_git`; the same call appears at line 12 in `clean.py`, `fetch.py`, `report.py`, `sync.py`, and `tag.py`.
+- Reverse: `scripts/archive.py:4`, `scripts/clean.py:4`, `scripts/fetch.py:4`, `scripts/report.py:4`, `scripts/sync.py:4`, and `scripts/tag.py:4` each define their own `run_git`.
+- Error: lines 7–8 of every script convert a non-zero Git exit into `RuntimeError(proc.stderr.strip())`.
+- Data: lines 6 and 9 of every script pass `['git', *args]` to `subprocess.run` with captured text output and return stripped stdout.
+- Boundary: each script imports `subprocess` directly at line 1; there is no shared scripts module, and `tests/test_smoke.py:1` provides no behavior coverage.
+
+## Task DAG
+
+### Wave 1
+
+**W1-01 Establish the shared Git helper contract**  after: none
+- Files: `scripts/git_helper.py`, `tests/test_git_helper.py`.
+- Test: first write `test_run_git_returns_stripped_stdout`; it fails because `scripts.git_helper` does not exist, then add the shared function and cover the non-zero-exit `RuntimeError` path as the second behavior face.
+- Risk: success or error semantics could drift during extraction; preserve the exact Git argv construction, captured text streams, whitespace stripping, and stderr-based exception message.
+- agent-decided — place the helper in `scripts/git_helper.py` because every consumer is in `scripts/` and the repository has no broader application package.
+- agent-decided — keep one stateless function because the duplicated behavior has no state or extension point that justifies a class or configuration layer.
+
+### Wave 2
+
+**W2-01 Replace all six local helpers with the shared implementation**  after: W1-01
+- Files: `scripts/archive.py`, `scripts/clean.py`, `scripts/fetch.py`, `scripts/report.py`, `scripts/sync.py`, `scripts/tag.py`, `tests/test_git_helper.py`.
+- Test: first write `test_all_scripts_bind_the_shared_run_git`; after W1-01 it fails because all six modules still bind local functions, then migrate every consumer and parameterize checks that each `main()` still prints the shared result.
+- Risk: one consumer could be missed or import the helper differently; enumerate all six modules in one parameterized identity test and retain each existing `main()` body.
+- agent-decided — keep the imported name `run_git` in every consumer because it makes the migration a direct substitution and leaves each `main()` unchanged.
+
+Checkpoint: run one `branch-end` review after Wave 2; the cumulative implementation is at most eight files and well below 400 lines, so no earlier wave-end or after-task checkpoint is justified.
+
+## Questions asked
+
+① — what — You want the six tools’ duplicated version-control command runner replaced by one shared implementation, and when it is done you will be able to update that behavior in one place, run all six tools with the same successful output, and get the same clear failure behavior when a command fails. Is that right?
+
+## Risks
+
+1. Import behavior can differ between package-style imports and direct file execution; implementation must preserve the repository's existing invocation modes and add a regression test for each supported mode.
+2. A shared function increases the impact of any semantic regression; the focused success and failure tests must land before consumers switch to it.
+3. The six scripts are deliberately behavior-identical today; do not fold in unrelated per-script cleanup while removing duplication.
